# Literature review

Papers that frame the project. PDFs are in `papers/` (kept local and not pushed to GitHub, except the
open-access Anzia review; see `.gitignore`). The written review is Section 2 of `../report/Final_Report.pdf`.

| Paper | Takeaway for this project |
|---|---|
| **Anzia (2021)**, "Party and Ideology in American Local Government: An Appraisal," *Annual Review of Political Science* 24 | Reviews the shift from "local politics is distinctive" to "local politics is partisan," and argues the conclusion is premature: local governments handle issues (zoning, services, development) that map unevenly onto national party divides, and interest groups may be omitted variables. |
| **Gerber & Hopkins (2011)**, "When Mayors Matter," *American Journal of Political Science* 55(2) | RDD on close mayoral elections. Democratic mayors spend a smaller budget share on public safety, with little effect on taxes or social policy. Partisan effects show up where authority is least shared with state/federal government. |
| **Einstein & Glick (2018)**, "Mayors, Partisanship, and Redistribution," *Urban Affairs Review* 54(1) | Interviews and surveys of 72 mayors. Partisanship explains much of the variation in support for redistribution, and rarely varies with institutional or economic context. |
| **de Benedictis-Kessner, Jones & Warshaw (2025)**, "How Partisanship in Cities Influences Housing Policy," *American Journal of Political Science* 69(1) | RDD with 3,261 mayoral elections. A Democratic mayor raises multifamily permitting; council partisanship shows no effect. Effects concentrate where councils lack veto power over by-right development. The housing-price effect is suggestive but statistically insignificant. |
| **Ferreira & Gyourko (2023, NBER WP 31966)**, "Does Political Partisanship Affect Housing Supply? Evidence from US Cities" | RDD on close Republican-Democrat mayoral races paired with permits since 1980. Finds no partisan effect on single- or multifamily supply, consistent with cities following the median voter. |
| **Das, Sinclair, Webster & Yan (2022)**, "All (Mayoral) Politics Is Local?" *Journal of Politics* 84(2) | Compares Twitter rhetoric of mayors, governors, and members of Congress. Mayors discuss more local topics; nationalized rhetoric is higher in larger cities and lower for longer-serving mayors. |

## How this project fits
Prior RDD work finds mixed evidence on whether mayoral party matters for policy inputs (budgets, permits).
This project asks whether partisanship predicts forward-looking changes in housing prices, using a city/year
fixed-effects panel. Identification differs from the RDD papers (close elections); the estimates here are
descriptive associations.
